Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell** audit of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, with integration-test calibration applied throughout.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 1           | 3              |
| **Total**| **5**       | **7**          |

> **Integration-test calibration:** This class is unambiguously integration-scoped (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`, project name `*.IntegrationTests`). **Mystery Guest** and **Eager Test** were evaluated but correctly suppressed — the in-memory SQLite connection is hermetic, and no test exceeds the 4-method threshold.

---

## High-Severity Findings

### 1. Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. "Unknown Test")

**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` — lines 113-119

```csharp
// ❌ Exercises code but never asserts anything — always passes
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
```

**Risk:** This test gives false confidence — if `Insert` silently corrupts data or skips rows, it still passes.

**Fix:**
```csharp
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

---

### 2. Conditional Test Logic

**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` — lines 94-109

```csharp
// ❌ if/else means one branch is NEVER exercised for a given run
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Risk:** One assertion path is dead code. If the type hierarchy changes, the test silently shifts to the other branch with no warning.

**Fix:** Split into two deterministic tests, each arranging the specific type:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "VIP", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com") as PremiumUser;
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

### 3. Sleepy Test

**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` — line 87

```csharp
// ❌ Blocks thread for 3 seconds — non-deterministic and slow
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Risk:** 3-second wall-clock sleep makes the suite slow and is inherently racy — the delay may be too short on a loaded CI agent or wastefully long otherwise. Even in integration tests, a polling/wait pattern is preferred.

**Fix:** Replace with a polling loop or `Task.Delay` with a timeout:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout period.");
}
```

---

## Medium-Severity Findings

### 4. Assertion Roulette

**Location:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` — lines 35-38

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);          // which one failed?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

4 assertions, zero message parameters. When one fails in CI, the stack trace points to a line number but the failure reason requires reading source.

**Fix:** Add a descriptive message to each assertion:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Database should assign a positive ID");
```

---

## Low-Severity Findings

### 5. Lazy Test

**Tests:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`, `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors`, `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`

All three test `_repository.Insert(...)` as their primary action. This is acceptable for integration tests covering distinct scenarios (single, bulk, concurrent), but worth noting — if Insert breaks, three tests fail simultaneously with overlapping diagnostics.

**Action:** No change needed. Awareness is sufficient.

---

## Smells Evaluated and NOT Found (14 of 19)

| # | Smell | Verdict |
|---|-------|---------|
| 2 | **Mystery Guest** | ✅ Clean — in-memory SQLite is hermetic (calibrated for integration scope) |
| 5 | **Eager Test** | ✅ Clean — no test exceeds 3 production method calls (calibrated for integration scope) |
| 6 | **Magic Number Test** | ✅ Clean — all numeric literals in assertions (`3`, `10`) match items just added |
| 7 | **Sensitive Equality** | ✅ Clean — no `ToString()` comparisons |
| 8 | **Exception Handling** | ✅ Clean — no `try`/`catch` blocks in tests |
| 9 | **General Fixture** | ✅ Clean — `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test |
| 10 | **Ignored Test** | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` or skip markers |
| 11 | **Duplicate Assert** | ✅ Clean — no identical assertion repeated |
| 12 | **Constructor Initialization** | ✅ Clean — uses `[TestInitialize]` properly |
| 13 | **Default Test** | ✅ Clean — class name is descriptive |
| 14 | **Redundant Print** | ✅ Clean — no `Console.WriteLine` / `Debug.WriteLine` |
| 15 | **Redundant Assertion** | ✅ Clean — no tautological assertions |
| 16 | **Resource Optimism** | ✅ Clean — database is created fresh per test |
| 17 | **Empty Test** | ✅ Clean — all tests have executable code |

---

## Positive Observations

- **Proper test isolation** — each test gets a fresh in-memory database via `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]`. No shared state leaks.
- **Sealed test class** — follows MSTest v4 best practice.
- **Clear AAA structure** — most tests have clean Arrange/Act/Assert separation.
- **Good naming** — names like `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` clearly convey scenario and expectation.
- **Correct async pattern** — `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` properly `await`s `Task.WhenAll`.

---

## Prioritized Remediation

| Priority | Smell | Fix Effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** | Low — add one assertion | Eliminates false confidence |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** | Medium — split into two tests | Eliminates dead assertion path |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** | Medium — polling loop | Removes 3s delay + flakiness |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** | Low — add message strings | Improves CI diagnostics |
